A||N||4||87ECO5||Any lawnmower experts out there?||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||06-06-2001||08:26 AM||||My lawnmower died last night.  Wondering if any of you can suggest how to fix it.<P>First, some background ... our yard has a lot of tree roots from a maple tree that was removed last year.  On three separate occasions, while trying to cut the grass short, the mower blade caught one of those roots and sheered the flywheel pin.  The mower was repaired by a neighbor all three times.  After the third fix, the mower became very hard to start (while pulling on the cord, the mower would sometimes kick back so hard that it'd just about pull your arm out of the socket).  But, once started, it ran OK.<P>So, last night, I get the front and sides done.  While doing them, I notice that the mower sounds louder than normal (I could hear a distant "metallic" knocking sound) but seems to be running fine.  I get about three passes done on the back, and the mower stops.  Not abruptly, mind you, more like it gradually lost power, slowed down, then stopped.<P>I'm sure it has gas, as I filled the tank before starting.  I'm sure it has oil because I check it before each use.<P>The only thing I can think of is that I purchased gasoline for it last weekend and I got 93 octane instead of the 87 that I usually get.  Could I have burned up the motor?<P>BTW, not that it makes a difference, but it's a Sears Craftsman model (so I don't know who the actual manufacturer is).<P>Any help is greatly appreciated.  Thanks, folks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Loki||06-06-2001||10:44 AM||||What does it do now if you try and start it?  It could be a bad plug, I know something similar has happned to my Snapper and it was a plug....<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>www.cox-internet.com/fiero  !!!UPDATED!!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||thomas_l||06-06-2001||11:22 AM||||Does it smoke (flooding) when it dies? Is the governor linkage clean and moving right? When it dies will it start right back up? Do you have to let it cool? Is there a defective low oil switch or handle kill switch? It could be possible to damage the magneto with the sudden stops that sheared the driveshaft pin... but not likely.||||reg||5
Z||000003||WampusCat||06-06-2001||11:42 AM||||93 octane gas won't hurt it.  It doesn't burn any hotter than 87, just resists detonation better.  Check this guide to help diagnose the problem:  <A HREF="http://routbort.neuro.duke.edu/repairfaq/REPAIR/F_lmfaq.html#LMFAQ_034" TARGET=_blank>http://routbort.neuro.duke.edu/repairfaq/REPAIR/F_lmfaq.html#LMFAQ_034</A> <P>The kickback on starting and "metalic pinging" sound may indicate timing is off.  Maybe the hole for the flywheel pin/key has worn from being sheared so many times and is allowing the flywheel to move a little around the shaft?  Or maybe it's sheared again?<p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||06-06-2001||01:31 PM||||worst scenario, could he have bent the crankshaft, causing the rod to sieze up on it?||||reg||1
